insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,fact,source_note) values
('2 Kings 3:4','An ancient stone from Moab tells the story of King Mesha''s rebellion against Israel—from Moab''s own side! It even names King Omri of Israel. This gives us another kingdom''s record of the rulers behind the account, though the reports differ in their details.','Answers in Genesis, ''6. Archaeological Finds'': https://answersingenesis.org/archaeology/6-archaeological-finds/ ; Mesha Stele (Moabite Stone), Louvre Museum.'),
('1 Kings 16:23','A king mentioned here, Omri, appears in writing carved by a foreign kingdom. The Mesha Stele, made in ancient Moab, remembers his rule over Israel. It is a clue from Israel''s neighbors that the kingdom in Kings belonged to real history.','Answers in Genesis, ''6. Archaeological Finds'': https://answersingenesis.org/archaeology/6-archaeological-finds/ ; Mesha Stele, Louvre Museum.'),
('Matthew 26:57','Archaeologists discovered an ancient bone box bearing a form of the name Joseph Caiaphas. Many researchers connect it with the high priest mentioned in the trial of Jesus, although the identification cannot be proven beyond doubt.','Answers in Genesis, ''6. Archaeological Finds'': https://answersingenesis.org/archaeology/6-archaeological-finds/ ; Caiaphas ossuary, Israel Antiquities Authority; identification is probable rather than conclusive.'),
('John 18:29','Pilate was a Roman governor, not merely a name in the Gospels. A stone inscription found at Caesarea gives his name and Roman title. The artifact confirms the official existed, although it does not describe this conversation with Jesus.','Answers in Genesis, ''6. Archaeological Finds'': https://answersingenesis.org/archaeology/6-archaeological-finds/ ; Pilate Stone, Caesarea archaeological find.')
